{
  "id": "grammar.a2.01.02.irregulares",
  "title": "Common Irregular Participles",
  "sections": [
    {
      "type": "text",
      "content": "Several common verbs have irregular past participles. They are used with the same forms of haber as regular participles. The most useful ones at this stage are *hecho*, *visto*, *escrito*, *puesto* and *dicho*. Learn the participle as part of the verb because it does not follow the regular -ado or -ido pattern."
    },
    {
      "type": "examples",
      "items": [
        {
          "spanish": "He visto las montañas.",
          "english": "I have seen the mountains."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Han hecho muchas fotos.",
          "english": "They have taken many photos."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "He escrito un mensaje.",
          "english": "I have written a message."
        },
        {
          "spanish": "Hemos puesto las mochilas aquí.",
          "english": "We have put the backpacks here."
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "table",
      "rows": [
        [
          "hacer → hecho",
          "done / made"
        ],
        [
          "ver → visto",
          "seen"
        ],
        [
          "escribir → escrito",
          "written"
        ],
        [
          "poner → puesto",
          "put"
        ],
        [
          "decir → dicho",
          "said"
        ]
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "tip",
      "content": "The auxiliary is still regular: he *visto*, has visto, ha visto, *hemos* visto, *han* visto."
    },
    {
      "type": "external-link",
      "topic": "the pretérito perfecto",
      "site": "Lingolia",
      "url": "https://www.lingolia.com/en/grammar/tenses/preterito-perfecto"
    }
  ]
}
